# Weekly Project Feedback Tool — Backlog

## 1. Project setup
Goal: An empty Next.js project with a passing test.
Description: Scaffold a Next.js (App Router, TypeScript) project, add a test runner, and write one trivial test that passes. Set up linting/formatting config so future tasks have a consistent baseline.

## 2. Database schema
Goal: Core tables exist in Supabase and are migratable.
Description: Create Postgres migrations for `projects`, `questions`, `weekly_cycles`, `responses`, and `digests`, covering the fields implied by the scope (schedule, deadline, attribution mode, recipients). Include a way to run migrations locally and document the schema.

## 3. Dashboard auth
Goal: A project lead can log in to the dashboard.
Description: Wire up Supabase auth (email/password or magic link) for the Next.js app, with a protected route group that redirects unauthenticated users to a login page. No project-specific logic yet — just working sign-in/sign-out.

## 4. Project CRUD in dashboard
Goal: A logged-in lead can create, view, and edit projects.
Description: Build dashboard pages/forms to create a project (name + basic metadata), list existing projects, and edit one. Persists to the `projects` table from Task 2.

## 5. Question configuration
Goal: A lead can define the weekly questions for a project.
Description: Add a dashboard screen to add/edit/remove questions tied to a project, stored in the `questions` table. Questions are free text with an order/position field.

## 6. Schedule configuration
Goal: A lead can set when a project's weekly cycle fires and its deadline.
Description: Add a form on the project settings page to configure send day/time and response deadline, saved on the `projects` record. Validate that the deadline is after the send time.

## 7. Recipients and attribution configuration
Goal: A lead can set who receives the weekly prompt and whether responses are named or anonymous.
Description: Add a dashboard UI to manage a list of recipients (Slack user identifiers) per project, and a toggle for attribution mode (named/anonymous), both persisted on the project.

## 8. Slack app registration and OAuth install
Goal: The Slack app can be installed into a workspace and store credentials.
Description: Create the Slack app manifest (bot scopes for DMs and posting to channels), implement the OAuth install flow, and store the resulting workspace token securely so later tasks can call the Slack API.

## 9. Slack bot: send weekly prompts
Goal: The bot can DM a project's questions to its recipients on demand.
Description: Write a function that, given a project's questions and recipient list, sends each recipient a Slack DM with the questions for that week and creates the corresponding `weekly_cycles` record. Trigger it manually (e.g. via a script or API route) for now — no scheduler yet.

## 10. Slack bot: collect responses
Goal: Replies to the weekly prompt are captured as responses.
Description: Handle incoming Slack messages/interactions from the DM in Task 9, matching them to the open `weekly_cycles` record and question, and storing them in the `responses` table.

## 11. Scheduler: weekly trigger
Goal: Weekly prompts fire automatically at each project's configured time.
Description: Add a Vercel Cron (or Supabase scheduled function) that runs periodically, finds projects due for their weekly send based on Task 6's schedule, and invokes the send logic from Task 9.

## 12. Scheduler: reminders
Goal: Non-respondents get a reminder before the deadline.
Description: Add a scheduled job that checks each open `weekly_cycles` record close to its deadline, finds recipients without a stored response, and sends them a Slack reminder DM.

## 13. LLM digest generation
Goal: A week's raw responses become a written summary.
Description: Write a function that takes all responses for a `weekly_cycles` record and calls the Claude API to produce a digest summarizing progress, blockers, and risks, then stores the result in the `digests` table.

## 14. Attribution handling in diges§Goal: Digests respect the project's named/anonymous setting.
Description: Update digest generation (Task 13) so that when a project is set to anonymous, responses are stripped of author identity before being sent to the LLM and before being displayed anywhere.

## 15. Digest delivery to Slack
Goal: The finished digest reaches the broader group automatically.
Description: After a digest is generated, post it to the project's configured Slack channel (add a channel field to the project if not already present), formatted for readability.

## 16. Dashboard: latest digest view
Goal: A lead (or viewer) can read the current digest in the dashboard.
Description: Add a project page that displays the most recent digest from the `digests` table, including which cycle it belongs to and response counts.

## 17. Dashboard: trends view
Goal: A lead can see how digests have changed over past weeks.
Description: Add a view listing past digests for a project in chronological order, so a lead can scan how progress/blockers evolved week over week. Simple list/timeline is sufficient — no charting required yet.
